WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:03.480
Every pod gets a new address when it restarts.

2
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:05.280
So how does one app find another?

3
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.390
You cannot store an address.

4
00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:12.790
This port has three answers: the Service, the load balancer, and the Gateway.

5
00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:15.225
First: a name that does not move.

6
00:00:15.245 --> 00:00:19.260
A Service has one stable address and one name in the cluster.

7
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:23.670
It finds its pods by label, and sends each request to a ready pod.

8
00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.095
No pods behind it?

9
00:00:25.115 --> 00:00:28.820
Then the label does not match, or no pod is ready.

10
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.455
Second: reach it from outside the cluster.

11
00:00:31.475 --> 00:00:33.117
Port-forward for a quick look.

12
00:00:33.137 --> 00:00:34.792
NodePort for a fixed port.

13
00:00:34.812 --> 00:00:37.240
LoadBalancer for a real address.

14
00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.753
One load balancer per app costs a lot.

15
00:00:39.773 --> 00:00:42.865
So put one front door before many apps.

16
00:00:42.885 --> 00:00:45.375
Third: route by host and by path.

17
00:00:45.395 --> 00:00:47.175
An Ingress is only rules.

18
00:00:47.195 --> 00:00:49.710
A controller must run to read them.

19
00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:51.935
The Gateway API is the newer way.

20
00:00:51.955 --> 00:00:54.910
It can also split traffic by weight.

21
00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:56.970
Quick recap of networking.

22
00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:59.057
Services find pods by label.

23
00:00:59.077 --> 00:01:01.307
Empty endpoints mean a label problem.

24
00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:03.180
One door, many routes.

25
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.300
Now start the first lesson below.
